    "q-61151": {
      "dom_id": "step-q61151",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "In Maria's class, there are $30$ children. Of this amount, $10%$ walk to school. How many children walk to school?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of children in Maria's class, which is $30.$ The percentage is $10%,$ and the part is the number of children who walk to school.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n10%=10÷100=0.1\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =10%\\times 30 \\\\  & =0.1\\times 30 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%$ of $30$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ children walk to school.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n10%=\\frac{10}{100}=\\frac{10÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =10%\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\times 30 \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{10} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%$ of $30$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ children walk to school.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
