    "q-93016": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93016",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A machine produces $200$ light bulbs per day. Of this amount, $1.5%$ of the bulbs are defective. How many bulbs made each day are defective?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of bulbs produced, which is $200.$ The percentage is $1.5%,$ and the part is the number of defective bulbs.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n1.5%=1.5÷100=0.015\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.015\\times 200 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n1.5%=\\frac{1.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{100}\\times 200 \\\\  & =1.5\\times \\frac{200}{100} \\\\  & =1.5\\times 2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day.",
