    "q-93082": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93082",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A rock band performed at two sold-out concerts. The first concert took place in a location with a $10,000$ people capacity. If the second location's capacity was $115%$ of the first location's capacity, what is the capacity of the second location?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first location, which is $10,000$ people. The percentage is $115%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second location.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n115%=115÷100=1.15\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =1.15\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n115%=\\frac{115}{100}=\\frac{115÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{23}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{20}\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =23\\times \\frac{10,000}{20} \\\\  & =23\\times 500 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
