    "q-93079": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93079",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "A restaurant originally had enough capacity to serve $80$ customers. Following a renovation, the restaurant increased its capacity to $120%$ of the original. What was the capacity of the restaurant after the renovation?",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the original number of customers the restaurant was able to receive, which is $80$ customers. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of customers the restaurant can receive after the renovation.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 80 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 80 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 16 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
